      "motivation": "The original query asks how matter can be programmed into living materials. Prior work has embedded living cells in hybrid sol-gel matrices [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4220814513), created stable protein-polymer biohybrid interfaces with PenTag [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4377098401), introduced metabolite-responsive phase-separated synthetic organelles for intracellular transcriptional control [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4402617058), and used photodegradable hydrogels to direct bacterial transport and delivery [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4413929537). However, these advances remain fragmented: molecular conjugation, intracellular sensing, and extracellular light-driven actuation are not coordinated by a predictive closed-loop programming layer, which is a central bottleneck for reliable living-material behavior. Control-systems engineering provides a direct solution: distributed-parameter process control treats spatially extended, coupled chemical and biological fields as controllable dynamical systems [Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W7049026970), and robust feedback design can maintain biological function despite disturbances [Author et al., 2020](https://openalex.org/W3214605415). Formal cyber-physical refinement can further translate high-level spatiotemporal goals into verifiable module constraints [Author et al., 2018](https://openalex.org/W2885089758). The proposed idea therefore programs matter into a living material by closing the loop between covalent cell-matrix interfacing, intracellular metabolite sensing, and light-patterned matrix remodeling.",
