  "ideas": [
    {
      "title": "Event-Triggered Living Material Repair via Cleavage-Sensitive PenTag Anchors and Phase-Separation Synthetic Organelles",
      "motivation": "Hybrid sol-gel materials already show that living microorganisms can be incorporated into inorganic/organic matrices [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4220814513). Recent work has separately solved key subproblems: PenTag enables site-specific covalent protein-polymer biohybrid conjugation [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4377098401); phase-separated synthetic organelles enable metabolite-responsive transcriptional control [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4402617058); and photodegradable hydrogels enable light-patterned bacterial transport and delivery [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4413929537). The unresolved bottleneck is not another attachment or actuation module, but an embodied programming rule that converts local material damage into constrained biological remodeling without continuous external computation. I propose to transfer event-triggered cyber-physical control [Author et al., 2018](https://openalex.org/W2808897733) and constrained control [Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W3092255269) into the material itself: a cleavage-sensitive PenTag anchor creates an optically readable material-state variable, a phase-separated organelle acts as a thresholded event detector and constraint enforcer, and a PenTag-conjugated repair effector writes new crosslinks into the matrix. This directly addresses the query by programming matter with a local living control loop rather than only adding living parts to matter.",
      "method_overview": "This method is intended to solve the lack of an embodied material-state variable and closed-loop programming in photodegradable living materials; it can be divided into four parts: a photodegradable living matrix, a cleavage-sensitive FRET PenTag anchor that creates a local damage state, a phase-separated synthetic organelle that threshold-gates the damage signal, and a PenTag-conjugated repair effector that writes crosslinks back into the matrix. The primary hypothesis is that the cleavage-sensitive anchor state, when coupled to the organelle, localizes repair and improves bacterial transport control relative to an otherwise identical non-cleavable anchor system.",
      "proposed_method": [
        {
          "name": "Photodegradable living matrix with damage-reporting capacity",
          "description": "The base material is a hybrid living sol-gel/hydrogel composite containing microorganisms [Author et al., 2022](https://openalex.org/W4220814513) and photocleavable crosslinks of the type used to direct bacterial movement, tune bacterial speed, and control delivery through light-patterned degradation [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4413929537). The matrix is further functionalized with PenTag-compatible reactive handles so that protein components can be covalently attached rather than passively adsorbed [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4377098401). Light therefore creates both a transport path and a local damage signal that can be sensed by the embedded living component."
        },
        {
          "name": "Cleavage-sensitive, FRET-readable PenTag anchor as a distinct material-state variable",
          "description": "PenTag is used to site-specifically and covalently conjugate a protein anchor module to the hydrogel, avoiding nonspecific binding and unstable non-covalent attachment [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4377098401). The anchor module contains a FRET pair and a photocleavable or tension-sensitive linker that releases a small synthetic ligand L when the local matrix is damaged or mechanically strained. Cleavage changes the FRET ratio, making anchor state optically readable, while released L provides a local chemical event signal. This creates a distinct state/control variable not present in the original references: a quantifiable PenTag anchor state that reports damage and can be tuned by light dose, linker chemistry, or mechanical loading."
        },
        {
          "name": "Phase-separated synthetic organelle as an event-triggered constrained controller",
          "description": "Engineered bacteria contain synthetic organelles adapted from metabolite-responsive, phase-separation-based transcriptional control [Author et al., 2024](https://openalex.org/W4402617058). The ligand-binding domain is retargeted to recognize ligand L released from cleaved PenTag anchors. At low L, condensates sequester a transcriptional activator or effector regulator, keeping repair off; when L exceeds a threshold, condensate reorganization releases the activator and induces expression of the repair effector. This implements threshold-based event-triggered actuation, adapted from event-triggered cyber-physical systems [Author et al., 2018](https://openalex.org/W2808897733), while finite sequestration capacity, refractory condensate reassembly, and viability-dependent expression implement hard constraints on actuation, adapted from constrained control theory [Author et al., 2008](https://openalex.org/W3092255269)."
        },
        {
          "name": "PenTag-conjugated repair writer that closes the local loop",
          "description": "The induced repair effector is a PenTag-fused crosslinking protein or enzyme that becomes covalently incorporated into the hydrogel through PenTag-mediated conjugation [Author et al., 2023](https://openalex.org/W4377098401). Because expression is triggered by local anchor cleavage and covalent capture occurs near damaged regions, the system writes new matrix crosslinks where damage is detected. The repaired region then restores local mechanical constraints and confines bacterial transport according to the original photodegradable transport program [Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4413929537). The closed loop is therefore: light or mechanical damage cleaves PenTag anchors, released ligand triggers phase-separated organelle control, and PenTag-conjugated repair locally restores matrix function."
        }
      ],
